ces "answer-2986023f-c3b0-5d34-89e7-d5b18cc1b181"] ["answer-field-1a59751e-949f-5d36-80c1-fa6f0759ee0d" :answer-field/choices "answer-660039ee-808b-5909-a6a4-855d47eca687"] ["answer-field-1a59751e-949f-5d36-80c1-fa6f0759ee0d" :answer-field/choices "answer-c054db7d-8b25-59ff-85a1-fc53976cbd21"] ["question-6c4bc11a-cdaa-550d-94a0-3ff2e50cf498" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-1a59751e-949f-5d36-80c1-fa6f0759ee0d"] [17592186100924 :knowledge-point/questions "question-6c4bc11a-cdaa-550d-94a0-3ff2e50cf498"] ["question-8f164f90-e76a-54c1-a336-782b45d61bdf" :question/id #uuid "8f164f90-e76a-54c1-a336-782b45d61bdf"] ["question-8f164f90-e76a-54c1-a336-782b45d61bdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-352756"] ["question-8f164f90-e76a-54c1-a336-782b45d61bdf" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{(-1)}^{n}\\,n!}{(n+4)!}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{6}$ is the sixth partial sum of $S.$ According to the alternating series error bound, $|S-{s}_{6}|<k$ for some real number $k.$ Find the value of $k.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}"] ["question-8f164f90-e76a-54c1-a336-782b45d61bdf" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{n!}{(n+4)!}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{(-1)}^{n}\\,n!}{(n+4)!}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is positive and decreasing for $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $6$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{6}|<|{b}_{7}|=|\\frac{{(-1)}^{7}\\,7!}{(7+4)!}|=\\frac{7!}{11!}=\\frac{1}{8\\cdot 9\\cdot 10\\cdot 11}=\\frac{1}{7920}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{1}{7920}.$"] ["question-8f164f90-e76a-54c1-a336-782b45d61bdf" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-159b45da-df98-533e-a437-a9180419cd3d" :answer-field/id #uuid "159b45da-df98-533e-a437-a9180419cd3d"] ["answer-cc145cd2-9498-5331-8842-8de1cc06800b" :answer/id #uuid "cc145cd2-9498-5331-8842-8de1cc06800b"] ["answer-cc145cd2-9498-5331-8842-8de1cc06800b" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-cc145cd2-9498-5331-8842-8de1cc06800b" :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7920}"] ["answer-field-159b45da-df98-533e-a437-a9180419cd3d" :answer-field/key "field-1"] ["answer-field-159b45da-df98-533e-a437-a9180419cd3d" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank] ["answer-field-159b45da-df98-533e-a437-a9180419cd3d" :answer-field/correct "answer-cc145cd2-9498-5331-8842-8de1cc06800b"] ["answer-field-159b45da-df98-533e-a437-a9180419cd3d" :answer-field/choices "answer-cc145cd2-9498-5331-8842-8de1cc06800b"] ["question-8f164f90-e76a-54c1-a336-782b45d61bdf" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-159b45da-df98-533e-a437-a9180419cd3d"] [17592186100924 :knowledge-point/questions "question-8f164f90-e76a-54c1-a336-782b45d61bdf"] ["question-b6f1577d-5608-56ec-bbab-86d743866f2e" :question/id #uuid "b6f1577d-5608-56ec-bbab-86d743866f2e"] ["question-b6f1577d-5608-56ec-bbab-86d743866f2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-352762"] ["question-b6f1577d-5608-56ec-bbab-86d743866f2e" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{n({n}^{2}+4)}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{i}$ is the $i$th partial sum of $S,$ use the alternating series error bound to determine an inequality that gives the values of $i$ such that\n$|S-{s}_{i}|<0.05.$\n\n${i}^{3}+3{i}^{2}+7i+5\\ge$\n{{field-1}}"] ["question-b6f1577d-5608-56ec-bbab-86d743866f2e" :question/worked-solution "First, let\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{3}{n({n}^{2}+4)}\\,\\text{and}\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{n({n}^{2}+4)}.\n$$\n\nThen ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing for $n\\ge 2$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the series is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\nBecause the series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $i$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{i}|<|{b}_{i+1}|.\n$$\n\nThus, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{b}_{i+1}|=|\\frac{3(-1)^{i+2}}{(i+1)((i+1)^{2}+4)}| & \\le 0.05 \\\\ \\frac{3}{(i+1)((i+1)^{2}+4)} & \\le \\frac{1}{20} \\\\ 60 & \\le (i+1)((i+1)^{2}+4) \\\\ 60 & \\le (i+1)({i}^{2}+2i+1+4) \\\\ 60 & \\le (i+1)({i}^{2}+2i+5) \\\\ 60 & \\le {i}^{3}+3{i}^{2}+7i+5 \\\\ {i}^{3}+3{i}^{2}+7i+5 & \\ge 60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-b6f1577d-5608-56ec-bbab-86d743866f2e" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-fdd473d7-ac4e-56be-8ce8-60ffb0f1e888" :answer-field/id #uuid "fdd473d7-ac4e-56be-8ce8-60ffb0f1e888"] ["answer-6fbc8956-7af9-5d82-88ed-b5cd5cfd7c9c" :answer/id #uuid "6fbc8956-7af9-5d82-88ed-b5cd5cfd7c9c"] ["answer-6fbc8956-7af9-5d82-88ed-b5cd5cfd7c9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-6fbc8956-7af9-5d82-88ed-b5cd5cfd7c9c" :answer/value "60"] ["answer-field-fdd473d7-ac4e-56be-8ce8-60ffb0f1e888" :answer-field/key "field-1"] ["answer-field-fdd473d7-ac4e-56be-8ce8-60ffb0f1e888" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank] ["answer-field-fdd473d7-ac4e-56be-8ce8-60ffb0f1e888" :answer-field/correct "answer-6fbc8956-7af9-5d82-88ed-b5cd5cfd7c9c"] ["answer-field-fdd473d7-ac4e-56be-8ce8-60ffb0f1e888" :answer-field/choices "answer-6fbc8956-7af9-5d82-88ed-b5cd5cfd7c9c"] ["question-b6f1577d-5608-56ec-bbab-86d743866f2e" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-fdd473d7-ac4e-56be-8ce8-60ffb0f1e888"] [17592186100925 :knowledge-point/questions "question-b6f1577d-5608-56ec-bbab-86d743866f2e"] ["question-fd9b72de-d1e8-5e4c-b443-057fdab0329d" :question/id #uuid "fd9b72de-d1e8-5e4c-b443-057fdab0329d"] ["question-fd9b72de-d1e8-5e4c-b443-057fdab0329d" :question/math-academy-id "q-352763"] ["question-fd9b72de-d1e8-5e4c-b443-057fdab0329d" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{(-1)}^{n+1}}{\\sqrt{n+1}}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{i}$ is the $i$th partial sum of $S,$ use the alternating series error bound to determine the values of $i$ such that $|S-{s}_{i}|<0.01.$\n\n$i\\ge$\n{{field-1}}"] ["question-fd9b72de-d1e8-5e4c-b443-057fdab0329d" :question/worked-solution "First, let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n+1}}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{\\sqrt{n+1}}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing for $n\\ge 1$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test. \n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $i$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{i}|<|{b}_{i+1}|.\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{(-1)^{i+2}}{\\sqrt{(i+1)+1}}| & \\le 0.01 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{i+2}} & \\le \\frac{1}{100} \\\\ \\sqrt[2]{i+2} & \\ge 100 \\\\ (i+2)^{1/2} & \\ge 100 \\\\ i+2 & \\ge (100)^{2} \\\\ i+2 & \\ge 10000 \\\\ i & \\ge 9998.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-fd9b72de-d1e8-5e4c-b443-057fdab0329d" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-3bf1afec-fd1f-50cb-988c-1d344a155a6e" :answer-field/id #uuid "3bf1afec-fd1f-50cb-988c-1d344a155a6e"] ["answer-b170842b-5b2d-5078-a5a6-acb218dd45e4" :answer/id #uuid "b170842b-5b2d-5078-a5a6-acb218dd45e4"] ["answer-b170842b-5b2d-5078-a5a6-acb218dd45e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-b170842b-5b2d-5078-a5a6-acb218dd45e4" :answer/value "9998"] ["answer-field-3bf1afec-fd1f-50cb-988c-1d344a155a6e" :answer-field/key "field-1"] ["answer-field-3bf1afec-fd1f-50cb-988c-1d344a155a6e" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank] ["answer-field-3bf1afec-fd1f-50cb-988c-1d344a155a6e" :answer-field/correct "answer-b170842b-5b2d-5078-a5a6-acb218dd45e4"] ["answer-field-3bf1afec-fd1f-50cb-988c-1d344a155a6e" :answer-field/choices "answer-b170842b-5b2d-5078-a5a6-acb218dd45e4"] ["question-fd9b72de-d1e8-5e4c-b443-057fdab0329d" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-3bf1afec-fd1f-50cb-988c-1d344a155a6e"] [17592186100925 :knowledge-point/questions "question-fd9b72de-d1e8-5e4c-b443-057fdab0329d"] ["question-8f270d15-6fe5-55df-829e-27dd74957fdc" :question/id #uuid "8f270d15-6fe5-55df-829e-27dd74957fdc"] ["question-8f270d15-6fe5-55df-829e-27dd74957fdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-352774"] ["question-8f270d15-6fe5-55df-829e-27dd74957fdc" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{{2(-1)}^{n}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n+1)}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{i}$ is the $i$th partial sum of $S,$ use the alternating series error bound to determine an inequality that gives the values of $i$ such that $|S-{s}_{i}|<0.2.$\n\n$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2i+3)\\ge$\n{{field-1}}"] ["question-8f270d15-6fe5-55df-829e-27dd74957fdc" :question/worked-solution "First, let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n+1)}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{(-1)}^{n}\\,2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n+1)}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing for $n\\ge 3$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test. \n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $i$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{i}|<|{b}_{i+1}|.\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{b}_{i+1}|=|\\frac{(-1)^{i+1}\\,2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2(i+1)+1)}| & \\le 0.2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2i+3)} & \\le 0.2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2i+3)} & \\le \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 10 & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2i+3) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2i+3) & \\ge 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-8f270d15-6fe5-55df-829e-27dd74957fdc" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-3de1ab9d-bf97-57ce-8fb8-7959134261a6" :answer-field/id #uuid "3de1ab9d-bf97-57ce-8fb8-7959134261a6"] ["answer-ccf8156a-5145-53a0-acb6-e6e427f7cdcf" :answer/id #uuid "ccf8156a-5145-53a0-acb6-e6e427f7cdcf"] ["answer-ccf8156a-5145-53a0-acb6-e6e427f7cdcf" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-ccf8156a-5145-53a0-acb6-e6e427f7cdcf" :answer/value "10"] ["answer-field-3de1ab9d-bf97-57ce-8fb8-7959134261a6" :answer-field/key "field-1"] ["answer-field-3de1ab9d-bf97-57ce-8fb8-7959134261a6" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank] ["answer-field-3de1ab9d-